Experiment: Creating and Running a Docker Container on Windows

Aim
To create a Docker image using a Dockerfile and run it as a container in Windows using Docker Desktop.
Software Required
Windows 10/11
Docker Desktop (WSL2 backend enabled)
PowerShell
 Theory
Dockerfile → Recipe to build image
Docker Image → Packaged application
Docker Container → Running instance of image
Flow: Dockerfile → Build → Image → Run → Container
Procedure:
Step 1: Verify Docker Installation
docker --version
docker info

Step 2: Create Project Directory
mkdir docker-demo
cd docker-demo

Step 3: Create Python Application
notepad app.py
Add:
print("HELLO WORLD! Docker is working correctly.")
Save and close.

Step 4: Create Dockerfile
notepad Dockerfile
Add:
FROM python:3.11-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY app.py .
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
Ensure file name is exactly Dockerfile (no .txt extension)
Step 5: Build Docker Image
docker build -t mydemo:v1 .
Verify image:
docker images
Step 6: Run Docker Container
docker run --name mydemo_container mydemo:v1
Expected Output:
HELLO WORLD! Docker is working correctly.

Step 7: View Containers
docker ps -a

Step 8: Cleanup (Optional)
docker rm -f mydemo_container
docker rmi -f mydemo:v1

Expected Result
Successfully created a Docker image and executed it as a container displaying Hello World message.
